<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomoyuki Yamashita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tiger of Malaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomoyuki Yamashita was a Japanese general who commanded the Twenty-Fifth Army in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1941 and 1942, earning the nickname the Tiger of Malaya. He later defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1944 and 1945. After the war a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military commission convicted him of war crimes committed by his troops, and he was hanged in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomoyuki-yamashita.jpg" alt="Tomoyuki Yamashita" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Japanese Army Photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamashita_Tomoyuki_(cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tomoyuki Yamashita was born on 8 November 1885, the second son of a local physician in a village in what is now Kōchi Prefecture on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. He graduated from the Imperial Japanese Army Academy in 1905 and fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 1914. He became an expert on Germany and served as assistant military attaché at Bern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> from 1919 to 1922. A leading member of the Imperial Way group, he became a rival of Hideki Tojo. After the February 26 Incident of 1936 he lost the trust of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> by appealing for leniency for the rebel officers, and he was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 6 November 1941 Yamashita took command of the Twenty-Fifth Army. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> on 8 December with a force about one third the size of the British forces, relying on speed, and the campaign ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> on 15 February 1942, when 30,000 front-line soldiers took 80,000 British, Indian and Australian prisoners. The occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> saw atrocities, among them the Alexandra Hospital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching massacres</a>. His responsibility is debated: the order to execute Chinese came, according to postwar testimony, from senior officers on his operations staff.</p><p>In July 1942 Yamashita was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, probably through Tojo's doing. On 10 October 1944 he took command of the Fourteenth Area Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. US forces landed on Leyte ten days later and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> on 6 January 1945. He did not declare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> an open city, and Rear Admiral Sanji Iwabuchi, against Yamashita's order, defended it; the fighting and Japanese atrocities killed more than 100,000 Filipino civilians. Yamashita surrendered on 2 September 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>An American military tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> tried him from 29 October to 7 December 1945 for atrocities against civilians and prisoners of war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, charging that he had failed to prevent them. He denied ordering or knowing of the crimes and said he was in effect charged with losing the war.</p><p>He was found guilty and sentenced to death. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> upheld the sentence, the supreme courts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declined to review it, and Justices Frank Murphy and W. B. Rutledge dissented, criticising the haste of the trial. He was executed on 23 February 1946. The principle that a commander is responsible for crimes of his troops that he did not try to stop is known as the Yamashita standard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomoyuki Yamashita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japanese troops landed at Kota Bharu on 8 December 1941 and advanced rapidly down the peninsula under General <b>Tomoyuki Yamashita</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-malaya">Japanese occupation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Akatsuka, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Los Baños, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.65,133.76&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Akatsuka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7965 943 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tomoyuki Yamashita"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8415 1213h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8415 1213h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.65,133.76&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
